# fix AI-tell prose
Site owners and agencies pay a developer to crawl their sitemap, score each page for AI-sounding prose, and present human-approved fixes rather than auto-publish them.
What would stop this: nothing!
The smallest thing worth building first: One SKILL.md (plus a small references/ folder) covering a first batch of the highest-confidence AI-tell patterns, each with detection criteria and a before→after replacement table, tested by running it against a handful of real pages and confirming a human reviewer would approve the rewrites. If it produces fixes you would actually sign off on, the crawl-and-score service wrapper can follow.
Do not build:
- An automated detector or linter-first tool — the rulebook prescribes fixes, it does not score first
- Auto-publishing fixes to live sites — everything is human-approved before it lands
- A hosted crawler or sitemap-scanning service in the first version
- A web UI or dashboard — the interface is the agent loading the skill
- Exhaustive coverage of all 29 patterns before the first ones are validated
- A proprietary license — the reference projects are MIT and the format is the proven convention


## How to work on this

These are defaults to start from, not rules. If you have a better way to reach the goal, use it and say why. Ask the person you are working for whenever the brief leaves something that changes the outcome.

**Size of the build: Full product.** A production-ready product: sound architecture, tests, security, monitoring and documentation, built in reviewed stages.

**Before writing code**
- Read `build-brief.md` end to end.
- Never build anything listed under "Do not build".
- Work in the phases of `docs/build-plan.md`, and check in with the person at the end of each one.

**Throughout**
- Keep commits small and the app runnable at every step.
- If the brief looks wrong or contradicts itself, say so instead of working around it.
- When the stop condition in the brief is met, tell the person before building anything more.